WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.599
Welcome to lesson two: scaling agents on Kubernetes.

2
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:06.418
Agent systems scale as two separate tiers,

3
00:00:06.468 --> 00:00:08.957
and confusing them is the classic mistake.

4
00:00:09.757 --> 00:00:12.971
At ten a.m., tickets for a stadium tour go on sale.

5
00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:17.283
The venue's help agent gets forty thousand questions in the first minute.

6
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:22.043
The autoscaler watches CPU, and CPU sits at twenty percent.

7
00:00:22.373 --> 00:00:24.427
Meanwhile the GPUs are saturated,

8
00:00:24.477 --> 00:00:27.678
requests queue, and fans wait a minute for a reply.

9
00:00:28.478 --> 00:00:31.785
The first tier is agent orchestration: CPU-bound,

10
00:00:31.835 --> 00:00:34.593
mostly waiting on I/O, and cheap to copy.

11
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:38.265
Keep it stateless, and scale it on request rate and concurrency.

12
00:00:39.065 --> 00:00:42.103
The second tier is model inference: GPU-bound,

13
00:00:42.153 --> 00:00:44.989
expensive, and usually the real bottleneck.

14
00:00:45.319 --> 00:00:50.111
Scale it on queue depth, batch occupancy, or a latency target, typically with KEDA.

15
00:00:50.911 --> 00:00:55.085
Even on the right signal, a new GPU replica can take minutes to start.

16
00:00:55.415 --> 00:01:00.187
It must pull a huge image, load weights into GPU memory, and load the engine.

17
00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:05.737
So pre-cache the model with the NIM Operator's NIMCache, so pods skip the download.

18
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:08.057
And keep a warm floor of replicas,

19
00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:11.777
because scale-to-zero is wrong for latency-sensitive services.

20
00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:16.729
Trigger scale-up at sixty to seventy percent capacity, not ninety-five.

21
00:01:17.059 --> 00:01:20.231
Then the new replica is ready before the rush, not after it.

22
00:01:21.031 --> 00:01:23.797
Sometimes one GPU must serve several workloads.

23
00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:28.188
MIG slices it in hardware, with dedicated compute and memory,

24
00:01:28.238 --> 00:01:30.413
for hard multi-tenant guarantees.

25
00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:35.003
Time-slicing has no isolation, so keep it for dev and test.

26
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:41.597
MPS offers partial isolation, and latency-critical inference gets a dedicated GPU.

27
00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:46.699
After the sale comes the bill, and the biggest saving is routing by difficulty.

28
00:01:47.029 --> 00:01:50.486
A small classifier sends easy questions to a small model,

29
00:01:50.536 --> 00:01:52.270
and hard ones to a large one.

30
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.284
Next, cache repeated answers and the shared system prompt.

31
00:01:56.614 --> 00:01:58.084
Then shrink the agent loop,

32
00:01:58.134 --> 00:02:02.025
distill a small model from production traces, and batch what can wait.

33
00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:06.701
Measure cost per successfully completed task, not cost per token.

34
00:02:07.031 --> 00:02:09.605
A cheaper model that fails and needs extra

35
00:02:09.655 --> 00:02:12.229
steps can cost more per resolved question.

36
00:02:13.029 --> 00:02:17.757
On the exam, never autoscale GPU inference on CPU utilization.

37
00:02:18.087 --> 00:02:22.709
Scale on queue depth or latency with a warm floor, then read on for the details.
